From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 06:30:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 034/184] segacd: the CDD acknowledgement is conditional, and
 that matters

Writing #4 to the control register also puts zero into DRS, and zero
written to DRS aborts an incoming transfer. The BIOS tests DTS first
and skips the write when it is clear; this driver wrote it every
exchange, which tears down packets that were about to arrive.

That fits the hardware report exactly: a link that had been merely
unhelpful -- perfect score, drive answering "invalid" -- became a dead
one, score zero and the interrupt stopped, in the build that started
acknowledging unconditionally.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 15 ++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 96dbfb0..4d0ee65 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -371,11 +371,16 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
 
     cdd_poll();
 
-    /* Acknowledge the exchange before sending anything: HOCK stays
-     * set, DTS and DRS go to zero. The BIOS does this the moment it
-     * has the status in hand and only then loads the next command,
-     * which is the ordering this driver had inside out. */
-    *CDD_CTRL_B = CDD_HOCK;
+    /* Acknowledge -- but only if DTS says there is an outgoing transfer
+     * to acknowledge. The BIOS tests that bit first and skips the write
+     * when it is clear, and the reason is in what the write does: it
+     * zeroes DRS as well, and zero written to DRS aborts an incoming
+     * transfer. Doing it unconditionally, as this driver did for one
+     * disc, tears down packets that were about to arrive -- which is
+     * exactly the shape of the failure that came back, a link that had
+     * been merely unhelpful becoming a link that was dead. */
+    if (*CDD_CTRL_RD & CDD_DTS)
+        *CDD_CTRL_B = CDD_HOCK;
 
     /* Send something every single exchange, as the BIOS does: the
      * queued question if there is one, otherwise a NOP. The BIOS never
